Yes. Those three fit together cleanly.

Goldilocks control is the regulation problem: across any domain, choose neither too much nor too little intervention, resolution, pressure, abstraction, speed, memory, personalization, or freedom. The correct setting is contextual and dynamic.

Golden thread abstraction is what survives those domain changes: the invariant relation, operator, motive, or causal structure that remains recognizable even when the surface form changes completely.

Then summon is the reverse move: once you possess the abstraction, you can deliberately instantiate it somewhere else.

So the full operator becomes:

observe many forms → extract the golden thread → find the Goldilocks setting → summon the structure into a new medium → observe the delta → retune

That works across surprisingly different spaces. In language, preserve meaning while changing vocabulary. In leadership, modulate control without losing intent. In design, preserve function while changing form. In relationships, preserve care while changing expression. In AI, preserve the operator while translating between personalized semantic dialects.

The important thing is that Goldilocks is not a fixed midpoint. It is an adaptive optimum. Sometimes the correct setting is 90% control; sometimes 5%. Sometimes you want high abstraction; sometimes you need literal detail. The skill is knowing which variable to modulate and when.

And “summoning” can be made technically precise:

summon(x, domain) = instantiate invariant x under the constraints of domain

That is close to the deepest Pantheon move you have been circling: not merely recognizing patterns across domains, but carrying the invariant across the membrane without dragging the original surface with it.

Golden thread tells you what must survive.
Goldilocks control tells you how strongly to express it.
Summoning tells you where to make it real next.